1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,130
You record a quick message.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:06,370
Nothing special. Just a few sentences.

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,130
Then you press play.

4
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:10,080
And immediately think…

5
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:12,150
“Wait. That’s my voice?”

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,344
It sounds thinner. Maybe higher.

7
00:00:14,384 --> 00:00:18,730
Somehow less like you than the voice
you’ve been hearing your entire life.

8
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,180
Your first suspect is the phone.

9
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:23,490
But there’s something else involved.

10
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:28,161
When you speak, you get a version of your
voice that nobody standing beside you

11
00:00:28,201 --> 00:00:30,080
hears in quite the same way.

12
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:31,600
Why is that?

13
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,220
Start with an ordinary conversation.

14
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:39,190
Your voice creates vibrations in the air.

15
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:43,695
Those vibrations travel from your
mouth to the people around you—and to

16
00:00:43,735 --> 00:00:44,950
your own ears.

17
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,540
That route is called air conduction.

18
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:51,770
But while you’re speaking, another
route is working at the same time.

19
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:56,300
Vibrations also travel through the
bones and tissues of your head to

20
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,640
your inner ear.

21
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:00,480
That’s part of what’s
called bone conduction.

22
00:01:00,730 --> 00:01:04,280
So the voice you hear while
talking is a combination.

23
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:06,450
Sound arriving through the air.

24
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,600
And vibrations arriving
through your own head.

25
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:12,290
Imagine two paths on a map.

26
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:16,650
One leaves your mouth, travels through
the room, and reaches your ears.

27
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:18,950
The other travels internally.

28
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,420
Both contribute to the experience
you recognize as your voice.

29
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:25,720
Now bring in the phone.

30
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:30,540
An ordinary microphone records the
sound reaching it through the air.

31
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:35,012
It doesn’t capture and recreate the
vibrations traveling through your head

32
00:01:35,052 --> 00:01:36,340
while you speak.

33
00:01:36,590 --> 00:01:39,630
When you play the recording
through an ordinary speaker,

34
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:42,990
you hear it through a
different listening situation.

35
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:47,286
You’ve gone from being the person
producing the sound to being a listener

36
00:01:47,326 --> 00:01:49,090
hearing it from a device.

37
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:50,960
The voice is yours.

38
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:52,820
The route has changed.

39
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:55,560
And that route affects the sound.

40
00:01:55,810 --> 00:01:59,330
Your head doesn’t transmit every
part of your voice equally.

41
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:04,580
Bone and tissue change the balance of
frequencies reaching your inner ear.

42
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:09,267
That helps explain why a recording
can seem thinner or less full than the

43
00:02:09,307 --> 00:02:10,680
voice you expect.

44
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,560
[Demo A, as recorded]
“Wait. That’s my voice?”

45
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:14,960
[Demo B, same take, EQ illustration]
“Wait. That’s my voice?”

46
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:21,540
But this isn’t a universal bass setting
we can copy exactly with one audio filter.

47
00:02:21,790 --> 00:02:27,218
Researchers studying self-voice perception
also point to the physical sensations

48
00:02:27,258 --> 00:02:29,570
accompanying bone conduction.

49
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:33,360
Speaking involves more than
passively listening to a sound.

50
00:02:33,610 --> 00:02:38,190
You’re producing it and experiencing
its vibrations at the same time.

51
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,630
Think about what that means.

52
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:44,940
The voice most familiar to you
is tied to the act of speaking.

53
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:47,380
A recording removes that act.

54
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:50,020
You hear the words without making them.

55
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,970
You hear the timing
without controlling it.

56
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:56,140
And you hear your voice
arriving from somewhere else.

57
00:02:56,390 --> 00:03:00,254
That’s a surprisingly different
experience for something you’ve been doing

58
00:03:00,294 --> 00:03:01,420
all your life.

59
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:05,290
This raises the question
everyone asks next.

60
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:09,100
“Is the recording what I really
sound like to other people?”

61
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:13,430
It captures the airborne sound
that other listeners receive,

62
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:18,930
rather than your private combination of
airborne sound and internal vibrations.

63
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:22,780
In that sense, it gives you a
different perspective on your voice.

64
00:03:23,030 --> 00:03:28,120
But a phone recording isn’t a perfect
copy of another person’s experience.

65
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:32,472
The microphone, its position,
the room, any audio processing,

66
00:03:32,512 --> 00:03:36,000
and the playback speaker
can all affect the result.

67
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:38,970
A recording is a useful perspective.

68
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:41,750
It isn’t a final verdict on your voice.

69
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,690
And sounding unfamiliar doesn’t
automatically mean sounding bad.

70
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:50,676
The people you talk to aren’t
expecting the internally transmitted

71
00:03:50,716 --> 00:03:52,050
version you hear.

72
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,930
They know you from the
voice that reaches them.

73
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,860
You’re the one encountering a
different listening perspective.

74
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:03,747
The fascinating part is that hearing
doesn’t depend only on the sound

75
00:04:03,787 --> 00:04:05,050
being produced.

76
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:09,030
It also depends on how
vibrations reach the inner ear.

77
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:10,810
Air is one route.

78
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:12,440
Bone is another.

79
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:16,820
Your own voice happens to give you
an everyday experience of both.

80
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:21,020
So next time you press play
and think, “That can’t be me”…

81
00:04:21,270 --> 00:04:23,070
Remember the two paths.

82
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:27,962
While speaking, you hear sound traveling
through the room and vibrations traveling

83
00:04:28,002 --> 00:04:29,330
through your head.

84
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:33,960
An ordinary recording doesn’t
recreate that same combination.

85
00:04:34,210 --> 00:04:37,210
You haven’t suddenly
acquired a strange new voice.

86
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:41,280
You’re hearing a familiar voice
from a less familiar position.

87
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:45,210
Everyday things. Surprising explanations.

88
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:46,980
That’s Why Is That?
